{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"agent_message","text":"To manage updates in your homelab, I have implemented an automated **Version-Tracking Update System** using Ansible. This approach leverages your existing \"rebuildable infrastructure\" philosophy by focusing on observability and automation rather than complex, error-prone rollback logic.\n\n### The Proposed Procedure\n\nThe system uses a dedicated Ansible playbook (`ansible/playbooks/update-tools.yml`) that can be run via cron or manually. It follows these steps:\n1.  **Version Discovery**: For each tool, it fetches the latest available version (via GitHub API, NPM registry, etc.) and compares it against a local \"source of truth\".\n2.  **Registry Tracking**: A JSON file (`/var/lib/ $\\text{homelab}$/versions.json`) is maintained on the target VM. This file tracks:\n    *   The `current_version` being used.\n    *   The `status` (e.g., `ok`, `failed`).\n    *   A timestamp of the last update.\n    *   Crucially, it preserves the **last known good version** if an update fails, allowing you to immediately re-provision or fix a \"bricked\" box with the correct version.\n3.  **Automated Verification**: After every update attempt, the system executes a `health_check` command (e.cmd). If the health check fails, it logs the failure in the registry and halts, preventing further automated damage while providing clear instructions for manual intervention.\n\n### Implementation Details\n\nI have added a new Ansible role and playbook to your repository:\n\n**New Files:**\n- `ansible/playbooks/update-tools.yml`: The main entry point defining which tools to manage (e.g., `opencode`, `openchamber`, `gh-cli`).\n- `ansible/playbooks/roles/tool_manager/tasks/update_tool.yml`: The core logic for version comparison, updating, and health verification.\n\n**Key Features of the implementation:**\n- **Zero manual intervention**: Once configured in the `tools` list, it handles everything from discovery to installation.\n- **Safe failures**: If an update breaks a service, the registry captures exactly which version failed (`status: \"failed\"`), so you know what to pin in your Terraform/Ansible code for a rebuild.\n- **Extensible**: Adding new tools is as simple as adding a new entry to the `tools` list with its respective `update_cmd`, `health_check_cmd`, and `get_version_cmd`.\n\n**Example of how a tool is defined in the playbook:**\n```yaml\n- name: \"openchamber\"\n  update_cmd: >\n    VERSION=$(curl -s https://api.github.com/repos/openchamber/openchamber/releases/latest | jq -r .tag_name | sed 's/^v//') &&\n    npm install -g https://github.com/openchamber/openchamber/releases/download/v$VERSION/openchamber-web-$VERSION.tgz\n  health_check_cmd: \"openchamber --version\"\n  get_version_cmd: \"curl -s https://api.github.com/repos/openchamber/openchamber/releases/latest | jq -r .tag_name | sed 's/^v//'\"\n```\n\nTo use this, simply run the playbook from your `ansible/` directory:\n```bash\nansible-playbook playbooks/update-tools.yml --ask-vault-pass\n```"}}
